<Legal
  title="Security overview"
  description="How Citerate is built and operated: architecture, access control, encryption, disclosure policy, and what we have not done yet."
  updated="Jul 12, 2026"
  version="1.2"
  draft={true}
  summary="Everything runs on Cloudflare's network with no long-lived servers to patch. Sessions are httpOnly and database-backed, secrets live in the platform's secret store, and every write is audit-logged. We are not SOC 2 certified yet and we say so rather than implying otherwise."
>
  <h3>Architecture</h3>
  <p>
    The site and API run as edge functions with no long-lived compute. Data lives in Cloudflare D1
    (SQLite) with rollups, and raw engine answer text in R2. There is no VPN, no bastion host, and no
    server for an attacker to log into.
  </p>

  <h3>Authentication and access</h3>
  <p>
    Passwordless email links and one-time codes, optional TOTP two-factor, and device trust. Sessions are
    stored in the database and referenced by an httpOnly, Secure, SameSite cookie — revoking a session is
    immediate. Internal access to production data requires 2FA and is limited to named staff; every
    privileged action is written to an audit log.
  </p>

  <h3>Encryption</h3>
  <p>
    TLS 1.3 in transit with HSTS preloaded. Data at rest is encrypted by the platform. Secrets are held in
    the platform secret store, never in the repository or in environment files.
  </p>

  <h3>Application security</h3>
  <p>
    Strict Content-Security-Policy, no inline third-party scripts beyond Turnstile and the analytics
    beacon, parameterised database queries throughout, Turnstile plus rate limits on every public form,
    and dependency review on each release.
  </p>

  <h3>Availability and backups</h3>
  <p>
    Daily database export with 30-day retention, restore tested quarterly. Deploys are atomic with instant
    rollback. Engine and queue status is published on the <a href="/changelog">changelog</a>.
  </p>

  <h3>Responsible disclosure</h3>
  <p>
    Report to <strong>security@citerate.com</strong>. We acknowledge within 24 hours and triage within 72.
    We credit reporters unless you prefer to stay anonymous, and we will not pursue legal action for
    good-faith research that respects customer data and avoids service degradation. Please do not include
    exploit details in the contact form — use the PGP key published at
    <code>/.well-known/security.txt</code>.
  </p>

  <h3>What we have not done yet</h3>
  <p>
    No SOC 2 Type II report, no ISO 27001, no third-party penetration test at the time of writing. Data
    residency and signed security exhibits are available on Enterprise. We would rather list the gaps than
    imply a certification we do not hold.
  </p>
</Legal>
